From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 19:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: show a disc's time for the picture on screen

A DVD's elapsed time comes from the input stream: the demuxer stamps each
packet with the navigator's time where it was read, and UpdatePlayState
took the step from dts to display time from the last packet read and
applied it to the clock. That is only right while the display time runs on
in step across everything queued. When the reader crosses a cell or title
boundary where the disc's time starts again, the picture on screen is still
the old one but it gets the new title's time, less the read-ahead, and the
new title's length.

The read-ahead is large: the queues are bounded by data size, which at a
DVD's bitrate is a minute or two. On one disc the time went from 0:02 to
-1:59 of 2:03 thirteen seconds in, then to -2:45 of 1:03,
while the picture played on. A negative time also means no resume point is
saved when the disc is stopped there.

The player now keeps an anchor each time that step (or the length) jumps
as it reads, and shows the time with the last anchor the clock has reached.
A step under a second is not a jump: the demuxer re-bases it on whichever
stream's packet follows each NAV pack, so it wobbles by the gap between
audio and video. A flush or a new file drops the anchors, a small step back
in dts drops only those after it, and the anchor in use is never the one
given up when they are full. The last packet read stays the fallback.

While an old title plays out before the next opens (01_08) there is no
demuxer and the play state stood still; the anchors still know the time of
the picture on screen, so the time and length now follow it there too. That
play-out can last a few minutes since 01_08, where the old wait gave up at
40 s.

When a new title set opens, the clock still holds the closed title's time
until the new streams sync, a few hundred ms: the time showed 5:11 of 2:22
for one reading at the start of a 2:22 title (stock shows the same). Until a
stream is in sync the time now goes by the first picture to come, which
also keeps the anchors from being dropped against a clock that is not the
picture's.

Only the time and length shown change. time_offset stays with the read
position: a DVD seek resolves its time in the title being read, and the
disc state saved with a resume point is the read position too, so both
behave as before.

This has to be in CVideoPlayer: it is the only place that sees both the
time a packet was read at and the clock that plays it.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff --git a/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/DisplayTimeAnchors.h b/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/DisplayTimeAnchors.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..52e0364
--- /dev/null
+++ b/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/DisplayTimeAnchors.h
@@ -0,0 +1,80 @@
+/*
+ *  Copyright (C) 2026 Team Kodi
+ *  This file is part of Kodi - https://kodi.tv
+ *  SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
+ */
+
+#pragma once
+
+#include "cores/VideoPlayer/Interface/TimingConstants.h"
+
+#include <cmath>
+#include <deque>
+
+/** The display time of what is on screen, for an input stream that numbers
+ * its own time (a DVD's cells and titles).
+ *
+ * The demuxer stamps each packet with the input stream's time where it was
+ * read, which runs ahead of the picture by everything queued. The player adds
+ * one anchor each time the step from dts to display time jumps, as it reads,
+ * and takes the last one the clock has reached when it shows the time.
+ *
+ * The demuxer re-bases that step on whichever stream's packet follows each NAV
+ * pack, so it wobbles by the few hundred ms between audio and video; a jump
+ * under a second is that, not a new title.
+ */
+class CDisplayTimeAnchors
+{
+public:
+  /** \param dts the packet's dts, in the player clock's time base
+   *  \param dispTime the input stream's time at the packet, in ms
+   *  \param totalTime the length of what is being read, in ms
+   */
+  void Add(double dts, int dispTime, int totalTime)
+  {
+    if (dts == DVD_NOPTS_VALUE || dispTime <= 0)
+      return;
+
+    const double offset = DVD_MSEC_TO_TIME(dispTime) - dts;
+    if (!m_anchors.empty())
+    {
+      // a step back in dts leaves the anchors before it to the pictures still queued
+      while (!m_anchors.empty() && m_anchors.back().dts >= dts)
+        m_anchors.pop_back();
+      if (!m_anchors.empty() && std::abs(m_anchors.back().offset - offset) < DVD_SEC_TO_TIME(1) &&
+          m_anchors.back().totalTime == totalTime)
+        return;
+    }
+    // the first is the picture's: when full, give up the one after it
+    if (m_anchors.size() == MAX_ANCHORS)
+      m_anchors.erase(m_anchors.begin() + 1);
+    m_anchors.push_back({dts, offset, totalTime});
+  }
+
+  /** Sets offset (display time minus clock) and totalTime for the picture
+   * at clock; false when no anchor is known.
+   */
+  bool Get(double clock, double& offset, int& totalTime)
+  {
+    while (m_anchors.size() > 1 && m_anchors[1].dts <= clock)
+      m_anchors.pop_front();
+    if (m_anchors.empty())
+      return false;
+    offset = m_anchors.front().offset;
+    totalTime = m_anchors.front().totalTime;
+    return true;
+  }
+
+  void Clear() { m_anchors.clear(); }
+
+private:
+  static constexpr size_t MAX_ANCHORS = 256;
+
+  struct Anchor
+  {
+    double dts;
+    double offset;
+    int totalTime;
+  };
+  std::deque<Anchor> m_anchors;
+};
diff --git a/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/VideoPlayer.cpp b/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/VideoPlayer.cpp
index cdbcbc8..369be37 100644
--- a/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/VideoPlayer.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/VideoPlayer.cpp
@@ -1571,6 +1571,8 @@ void CVideoPlayer::Prepare()
   m_processInfo->SetFrameAdvance(false);
   m_State.Clear();
   m_nextStream.Cancel();
+  m_displayTime.Clear();
+  m_nextStreamSyncing = false;
   m_CurrentVideo.hint.Clear();
   m_CurrentAudio.hint.Clear();
   m_CurrentSubtitle.hint.Clear();
@@ -1786,10 +1788,14 @@ void CVideoPlayer::Process()
             m_playSpeed == DVD_PLAYSPEED_PAUSE, std::chrono::steady_clock::now());
       if (state == CNextStreamDrain::State::PLAYING_OUT)
       {
+        UpdatePlayState(0);
         CThread::Sleep(100ms);
         continue;
       }
       m_nextStream.Cancel();
+      // the anchors left are all the old title's
+      m_displayTime.Clear();
+      m_nextStreamSyncing = true;
       const bool drained = state == CNextStreamDrain::State::DONE;
       if (!drained)
         CLog::Log(LOGWARNING, "VideoPlayer: next stream, old streams given up");
@@ -2822,6 +2828,13 @@ void CVideoPlayer::UpdateTimestamps(CCurrentStream& current, DemuxPacket* pPacke
   current.dts = dts;
 
   current.dispTime = pPacket->dispTime;
+
+  if (m_pInputStream &&
+      (&current == &m_CurrentVideo || (&current == &m_CurrentAudio && m_CurrentVideo.id < 0)))
+  {
+    if (CDVDInputStream::IDisplayTime* displayTime = m_pInputStream->GetIDisplayTime())
+      m_displayTime.Add(dts, pPacket->dispTime, displayTime->GetTotalTime());
+  }
 }
 
 static void UpdateLimits(double& minimum, double& maximum, double dts)
@@ -4901,6 +4914,8 @@ bool CVideoPlayer::CloseStream(CCurrentStream& current, bool bWaitForBuffers)
 void CVideoPlayer::FlushBuffers(double pts, bool accurate, bool sync)
 {
   m_syncStartPtsWait.reset();
+  m_displayTime.Clear();
+  m_nextStreamSyncing = false;
   CLog::Log(LOGDEBUG, "CVideoPlayer::FlushBuffers - flushing buffers");
 
   double startpts;
@@ -5894,9 +5909,28 @@ int ToVisibleChapter(const std::vector<int>& rawChapters, int rawChapter)
 
 void CVideoPlayer::UpdatePlayState(double timeout)
 {
-  // the old streams play out with no demuxer to read a position from
+  // the old streams play out with no demuxer to read a position from; only the time moves on
   if (m_nextStream.Pending())
+  {
+    double offset = 0.0;
+    int totalTime = 0;
+    if (!m_displayTime.Get(m_clock.GetClock(), offset, totalTime))
+      return;
+    SPlayerState state(m_State);
+    state.time = DVD_TIME_TO_MSEC(m_clock.GetClock() + offset);
+    state.timeMax = totalTime;
+    if (m_Edl.HasCuts())
+    {
+      state.time = static_cast<double>(
+          m_Edl.GetTimeWithoutCuts(std::chrono::milliseconds(std::lround(state.time))).count());
+      state.timeMax = state.timeMax - static_cast<double>(m_Edl.GetTotalCutTime().count());
+    }
+    m_processInfo->SetPlayTimes(state.startTime, state.time, state.timeMin, state.timeMax);
+    std::unique_lock lock(m_StateSection);
+    m_State.time = state.time;
+    m_State.timeMax = state.timeMax;
     return;
+  }
 
   if (m_State.timestamp != 0 &&
       m_State.timestamp + DVD_MSEC_TO_TIME(timeout) > m_clock.GetAbsoluteClock())
@@ -6040,8 +6074,30 @@ void CVideoPlayer::UpdatePlayState(double timeout)
 
         state.time_offset = DVD_MSEC_TO_TIME(dispTime) - state.dts;
       }
-      state.time += state.time_offset * 1000 / DVD_TIME_BASE;
-      state.timeMax = pDisplayTime->GetTotalTime();
+      // the packets just read can be a title ahead of the picture; a seek and the
+      // disc's state go by the read position, so time_offset stays with it
+      double offset = state.time_offset;
+      int totalTime = pDisplayTime->GetTotalTime();
+      // a title set opening after the old one played out: until its streams sync
+      // the clock still holds the old title's time, so go by the first picture to come
+      double clock = m_clock.GetClock();
+      if (m_CurrentVideo.syncState == IDVDStreamPlayer::SYNC_INSYNC ||
+          m_CurrentAudio.syncState == IDVDStreamPlayer::SYNC_INSYNC)
+        m_nextStreamSyncing = false;
+      if (m_nextStreamSyncing)
+      {
+        clock = m_CurrentVideo.startpts != DVD_NOPTS_VALUE ? m_CurrentVideo.startpts
+                                                         : m_CurrentAudio.startpts;
+        if (clock == DVD_NOPTS_VALUE)
+        {
+          clock = 0.0;
+          offset = 0.0;
+        }
+        state.time = DVD_TIME_TO_MSEC(clock);
+      }
+      m_displayTime.Get(clock, offset, totalTime);
+      state.time += offset * 1000 / DVD_TIME_BASE;
+      state.timeMax = totalTime;
     }
     else
     {
